$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537904 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a6241f6-827e-5f45-a2f2-3047ca1b82e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537904 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}18 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-48 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{38}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 18\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-48 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537968 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86762" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537968 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537968 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537968 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc66ebbe-2cca-5407-87bc-772df677089f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537968 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}17 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537995 #ed